{
  "dom_id": "step-q124711",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}, & x=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}, & x=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}, & x=\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{25}, & x=0 \\\\ \\frac{23}{25}, & x=1\\end{aligned}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0\\le f(x)\\le 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x)=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $f$ do not sum to $1:$\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}\\ne 1\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability mass function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5}\\le 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}=1.\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability mass function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{2}{25},\\frac{23}{25}\\le 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{25}+\\frac{23}{25}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"",
